<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Lhuyd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1660">1660</a>–<a href="/years/1709">1709</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Edward Lhwyd, Edward Llwyd</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Lhuyd was a Welsh naturalist, antiquary and linguist, keeper of the Ashmolean Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> from 1690. He travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and the other Celtic countries recording inscriptions, fossils and plants, and in 1696 copied the inscription of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pillar-of-eliseg">Pillar of Eliseg</a>. His Archaeologia Britannica (1707) first showed that the Celtic languages are related.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-lhuyd.jpg" alt="Edward Lhuyd" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Llywelyn2000 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Lhuyd.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
